    "updated_at": "2022-04-22T19:19:34Z",
    "body": "@domenic Thank you :) There are a few rabbits in this rabbit hole and a bit of some chicken and egg action.\r\n\r\nScreen readers announce what is exposed to them via the browser's accessibility tree / API. It is my understanding that browser manufacturers interpret the HTML specification to build features.\r\n\r\nCould it be that there is no grey area and things are very binary/boolean based on the non-normative definition of Validity State for an empty form field with the `required` attribute within the Spec as \"[Suffering from being missing](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/form-control-infrastructure.html#suffering-from-being-missing)\" as well as within the [The constraint validation API](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/form-control-infrastructure.html#the-constraint-validation-api:validity-states)?\r\n\r\nIs there room in the HTML specification to encourage checking if the field has been interacted with prior to defining validity for browser makers and assitive technology makers to run with? Or is that too opinionated?\r\n\r\n",
    "author_association": "NONE",
    "pin": null,
