    "updated_at": "2022-04-24T21:28:13Z",
    "body": "@domenic wrote:\r\n> It has nothing to do with what accessibility tech shows to their users.\r\n\r\nAs implemented in browsers the invalid state exposed in the browser accessibility tree is directly related to the `ValidityState` properties. \r\nWhat i could envisage is that a more nuanced relationship between the required state and the invalid state is implemented in the browser accessibility layer, so that for example, a control with a required state is only flagged as invalid (in acc tree) once a user has started to input data. i.e. when the value=\"\" its valid. when value=\"x\" (as long as x does not meet the validation constraints) its invalid. This does not resolve how to flag initial validity of checkbox/radiobutton that are required though.\r\n\r\nAs this effects how the states are implemented in the browsers accessibility APIs, it should be defined in the HTML AAM spec as @annevk alluded to in https://github.com/whatwg/html/issues/7848#issuecomment-1107372609\r\n",
    "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
    "pin": null,
